<!--
beet's own entry: the dev commands, wired as routes over the capabilities linked
into the `beet` binary.

The wasm test runner depends on it: `.cargo/config.toml` sets
`runner = 'beet run-wasm'`, so `cargo test --target wasm32-...` invokes
`beet run-wasm <module>`, which discovers this entry and dispatches the
`<RunWasm/>` route. The rest are convenience: build, check, static export, pdf,
s3 sync.

It is a one-shot `<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>` root with the `Router` as its
child: the binary declares only that this load should run itself, the root's
`CallOnReady` calls it with the process request, it hops down to the router, the
positional route arg selects the command, and the command resolves and the process exits.
`always=true` makes this dispatcher run on every boot regardless of `--server`, since
`--server` selects a *declared server's* transports, not this dispatcher's.

    cargo install --path crates/beet-cli --all-features   # then, from anywhere in the repo:
    beet run-wasm <module.wasm>            # run a wasm test module
    beet check examples/bsx_site           # lint a no-code site
    beet export-static examples/bsx_site   # write its dist/
    just build-wasm-min                    # the smallest browser runtime (assets/wasm/beet-min.wasm)
    just build-wasm-full                   # ..and the kitchen sink (assets/wasm/beet-full.wasm)

Serving a site is NOT a command here. A site entry is its own CLI, so it is
launched directly and names its own verb, exactly as its deploy verbs are
(`--main` takes a directory holding an entry document, or the entry file itself):

    beet --main=site serve --server=http       # the beet website (site/main.bsx)
    beet --main=site serve --server=tui        # ..as a live terminal
    beet --main=site serve --server=http,ssh   # ..http plus a multi-tenant ssh terminal
    beet --main=examples/bsx_site --server=http   # a zero-code BSX site, whose root IS the server

That is the identical path the deployed box takes (`app --store=s3://.. serve`),
and it is why the site's resources and deploy verbs live in `site/main.bsx`: the
application that runs on them is the thing that declares them (`just beet-deploy`
runs `beet --main=site deploy`).

What lives here is this WORKSPACE's own shared asset bucket
(`beet--shared--assets`), the source of record for the files too large for git
that the examples, tests and wasm builds read out of `./assets`. It is
public-read so a fresh checkout hydrates without credentials, and it is declared
under a `shared`-stage `<Stack>` because a source shared by developers is not
owned by any deploy stage. A resource belongs to the stack it is authored under
and to no other, so no stage deploy touches it.

    just beet-shared apply                 # provision the workspace assets bucket
    just beet-shared pull                  # hydrate ./assets from it (no credentials)
    just beet-shared push                  # publish ./assets to it

Structure is universal, so a lean `beet` (the default build, ie `beet run-wasm`)
loads that subtree too: the tags it never linked build as inert entities,
quietly, because the route declares `RequireFeatures(["infra","extra"])`. Dispatching
a shared verb in such a build fails naming the missing features. The stage rides
the `<Stack>` COMPONENT, registered in every native build, so `shared` is correct
even there; `<DeployRoutes/>` (a beet_extra template) adds the tofu lifecycle
routes, and is one of the tags a lean build has no behavior for.

The shared verbs need AWS credentials in `.env`, plus `tofu`.
-->
<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<PackageConfig title="Beet" app_name="beet" description="A creative tool engine built on bevy" version="0.0.9-dev.16"/>
	<RunWasm/>
	<BuildWasm/>
	<Check/>
	<AnalyticsReport/>
	<ExportStatic/>
	<ExportPdf/>
	<CaptureScreenshot/>
	<SyncS3/>
	<Route path="shared" {RequireFeatures(["infra","extra"])}>
		<Stack stage="shared">
			<S3BucketBlock label="assets" deploy_versioned=false public_read=true force_destroy=false/>
			<DeployRoutes/>
			<Route path="push" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="assets"/>
			</Route>
			<Route path="pull" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<DirSync bucket="assets" local_dir="assets" {SyncS3Bucket{direction:Pull, no_sign_request:true}}/>
			</Route>
		</Stack>
	</Route>
</Router>
</CliServer>
